  <div class="lh-container">
    
<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Tefilas Arvis [Laws of the Evening Prayer]<br>Halachah 4, Part 3 (§§ 18–28)<br>Based on <span class="lr">LM I:65</span> (<i>"Vayomer Boaz el Rus"</i>)</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 18                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 18</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"Hashem sat [enthroned] at the Flood"</span> — which is said at the end of the seven sounds that Dovid spoke over the waters, and which are the aspect of the sounds of the Giving of the Torah that was given with sounds, as explained in the <span class="h">seforim</span>: that this psalm speaks of the Giving of the Torah. For the Flood waters that came upon the world then — this was drawn from the aspect of excessive light, which causes the Breaking of Vessels, from which all judgments and troubles derive, G-d forbid. Meaning: the judgment then was exceedingly intense on account of their evil deeds, and they needed a <span class="h">bitul</span> in perfection until they would merit to nullify the judgment. And then they would have drawn the attainment of Torah — which is the aspect of waters — from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> that remains from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above. But because they did not merit this and did not arouse themselves to <span class="h">teshuvah</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above — therefore what should have been the aspect of the waters mentioned above, which are the aspect of the attainment of Torah, became instead the aspect of the waters of the Flood, because they had no vessels to receive the light, on account of the corruption of their deeds. Therefore it was, in their regard, in the aspect of excessive light — from which all judgments derive — and these are the waters of the Flood.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore our Sages of blessed memory said, in truth, on the verse <span class="v">"and the rain was upon the earth"</span>: that had they aroused themselves to <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, they would have been rains of blessing and generosity. Meaning, as mentioned above: had they merited <span class="h">teshuvah</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above — they could have received the attainment of the waters of Torah, which are the aspect of rains of blessing and generosity, in the aspect of <span class="v">"a rain of generosity You poured down, O G-d"</span> — which is said regarding the Giving of the Torah. But because they did not merit <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, what should have been the waters of Torah, the rains of blessing, became a Flood for them, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is what Rashi explains on the verse <span class="v">"for with them He judges the peoples"</span> — regarding rain: a parable of a baker etc. — that all punishments are through water and all blessings and bounty are through water, in the aspect of <span class="v">"He gives food abundantly."</span> Meaning, as mentioned above: the rains and waters are drawn from the aspect of the waters of <span class="h">da'as</span> and the supernal attainments that are compared to water, as it is written: <span class="v">"for the earth shall be full of the knowledge of Hashem, as the waters"</span> etc. Therefore there are two aspects in them — for good and for the opposite — depending on the recipients. For one who merits through his deeds to repair good vessels — he merits the attainment of the waters of <span class="h">da'as</span> through this. And the opposite for the opposite, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 19                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 19</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of what Noach merited afterward — through the offerings he attained to bring, as mentioned above — he merited that [Hashem] gave him the sign of the rainbow as an eternal protection, in the aspect of <span class="v">"I have set My bow in the cloud."</span> For the rainbow is the aspect of <span class="h">gunin nehirin</span> [luminous colors], the aspect of the light of the shining and sparkling drawn from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span> — through which is the essential sweetening, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"and I shall see it, to remember an everlasting covenant"</span> — <span class="v">"to remember"</span> specifically, the aspect of the remembrance mentioned above — the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, <span class="h">Yom HaZikaron</span>, which is the aspect of the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of the sounds of the holy <span class="h">shofar</span>, whose mnemonics are <span class="h">Kuf-Shin-Tav</span> [= <span class="h">Keshes</span>, rainbow], as is brought. This is the aspect of the sign of the rainbow, which is the aspect of the light of the shining of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> — which is the aspect of the <span class="h">shofar</span>, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of the reddening of the face of the one who blows [the <span class="h">shofar</span>], which is drawn from the supernal lights, as is brought. This is from the aspect of the light of the shining of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, from which all the lights and splendors and <span class="h">Sefiros</span> derive. And this is the aspect of the sound of the holy <span class="h">shofar</span>, the aspect of the luminous colors of <span class="h">Keshes</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 20                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 20</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"and it was, after seven days, that the waters of the Flood were"</span> etc. — and our Sages of blessed memory expounded: that He gave them a delay of the seven days of mourning for Mesushelach the righteous. For it is explained in the Torah mentioned above, at its beginning, regarding the <span class="h">Ba'al HaSadeh</span> who is engaged in the tikun of the Field — which is the tikun of the souls etc. — that this <span class="h">Ba'al HaSadeh</span> must be exceedingly, exceedingly exalted in his level, and must endure many troubles and suffering, G-d forbid, may the Merciful One save us. And there are times when he must pass away on account of this etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">For sometimes the judgment prevails so much that it is impossible to sweeten it through any <span class="h">bitul</span>, except through the actual passing of the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, G-d forbid. And then the world must arouse themselves greatly in <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, and look at themselves well — where they are in the world, what they caused through their sins, that the <span class="h">tzadik</span> passed away on their account. And this is the aspect of what the prophet cried out: <span class="v">"the righteous one has perished and no man takes it to heart; men of lovingkindness are gathered in, with none understanding that it is because of the evil that the righteous one is gathered"</span> etc. For when, G-d forbid, the <span class="h">tzadik</span> passes away, one must take it deeply to heart and understand that <span class="v">"because of the evil the righteous one is gathered"</span> — the essential cause of his passing is "because of the evil," because of the intensity of the judgment in the world, to the point that he could not nullify the judgment through any <span class="h">bitul</span> in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span> etc., as mentioned above. Rather, he was compelled to nullify himself in such a <span class="h">bitul</span> that he passed away completely.</div>

<div class="p">And then everything depends on the generation that remains after him: they must take deeply to heart the passing of the <span class="h">tzadik</span> and arouse themselves to complete <span class="h">teshuvah</span> through this. And then they merit to draw light from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">tzadik</span> that remains from him. For every <span class="h">tzadik</span>, when he passes away, certainly leaves behind in the world some good <span class="h">reshimah</span> through his Torah and good deeds. And then the essential sweetening is through the orphaned generation that remains after him: when they arouse themselves in <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, they draw a great light from his <span class="h">reshimah</span> — through which all the judgments in the world are sweetened and nullified. For because the <span class="h">tzadik</span> has passed away above and is now in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>, therefore a great and wondrous light is drawn from there each time to his <span class="h">reshimah</span> that remains in this world. But one needs at least some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sasa</span> [arousal from below] through the good deeds of the generation, to draw through this the light of his holy <span class="h">reshimah</span>. For through their good deeds — even though they are in the aspect of smallness and do not have the power to receive the attainment of Torah to sweeten judgments — nevertheless, through the power of the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who has passed away, they can through their good deeds draw a great and wondrous light from his <span class="h">reshimah</span> that now illuminates with a great and awesome light, because the <span class="h">tzadik</span> is in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span> in perfection.</div>

<div class="p">But in the days of the generation of the Flood, they did not merit to arouse themselves to <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. Therefore the judgment prevailed until the Flood came — which was made from the waters of <span class="h">da'as</span> that became a Flood etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 21                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 21</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of what Hashem said to Dovid HaMelech, peace upon him: <span class="v">"when your days are fulfilled"</span> etc. — that Dovid wished to build the House [Temple], and Hashem answered him that in his lifetime he would not merit this; only after his passing would his son build the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>. For the essential perfection of the attainment of Torah is in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where the aspect of the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span> resides — in the innermost sanctum, the <span class="h">Kodesh Hakodashim</span>, where the <span class="h">Kohen Gadol</span> enters once a year on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, as mentioned above. Where the <span class="h">Even HaSh'siyah</span> [Foundation Stone] stood, with the Ark and the Tablets — which are the generality of the Torah, for from there Torah goes forth etc.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential tikun of the worlds, drawn through receiving the Torah, was not completed until the House was built in the days of Shlomo. As stated in the Writings of the Ari"zal: in the days of Moshe the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> returned only in the aspect of <span class="h">achor b'achor</span> [back to back]. And they were not unified in the aspect of <span class="h">panim b'fanim</span> [face to face] until the days of Shlomo when the House was built etc. — see there. And this is the aspect of: <span class="mx">"on the day of his wedding"</span> — this is the Giving of the Torah; <span class="mx">"and on the day of the joy of his heart"</span> — this is the building of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>. For the essential perfection of the attainment of Torah — the aspect of the Giving of the Torah — is in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span> in the innermost sanctum resides, from which all Torah derives etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Dovid, who wished to complete this tikun — to build the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> where the essential aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> and the essential attainment of Torah resides etc. — Hashem answered him that a wondrous and awesome tikun such as this is impossible to accomplish in his lifetime. Rather, he must pass away first. And then, through his passing specifically, his son would merit to draw light from his <span class="h">reshimah</span> to build the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> and complete this entire tikun in perfection. This is as mentioned above. Therefore in truth the entire <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> is called by Dovid's name, as it is written: <span class="v">"see Your house, Dovid."</span> For everything is through him. For <span class="mx">"the righteous are greater in their death than in their lifetime."</span> And what he cannot complete in his lifetime, he completes after his passing specifically, as mentioned above — like Dovid HaMelech, peace upon him, regarding the building of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> where the essential <span class="h">bitul</span> resides, where the tikun of all the worlds takes place, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And so we find with Moshe Rabbainu, peace upon him, who wished to enter <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> and conquer it himself. And Hashem did not allow him and said: <span class="v">"do not continue to speak to Me further about this matter."</span> For to conquer <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, to take it from the seven nations and deliver it to Israel — this is the essential tikun, for there in <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> are all the holinesses and there is the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> where the essential <span class="h">bitul</span> resides etc. And to win such a war, especially at the outset, to take all of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> from the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> and restore it to holiness — on which everything depends — therefore even Moshe Rabbainu was unable to win this war in his lifetime. Only after his passing specifically. For specifically after his passing, Yehoshua his student conquered all of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> through the power of Moshe himself. Which is at first glance puzzling: <span class="mx">"if Rebbi did not teach it, from where does R' Chiya know it?"</span> If Moshe did not have the power to enter and conquer <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, how did Yehoshua his student merit this?</div>

<div class="p">But according to the above, it is very well understood. For certainly everything that Yehoshua conquered, Moshe himself conquered. For everything was through Moshe specifically. But a great matter such as conquering <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> at the outset etc. — even Moshe could not complete in his lifetime, only through his passing specifically. For specifically through his passing — that he merited the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>, passing away in the aspect of <span class="h">R'usa d'R'usin</span> [Will of Wills] etc. — through this specifically Yehoshua merited, through the power of his passing specifically, to conquer <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, to subdue the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>, to elevate holiness, and to repair all the worlds. For such a great and awesome tikun — which is the aspect of the tikun of the Supernal Field — Moshe needed to pass away specifically. And through this specifically the tikun was completed, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"upon my bed at night I sought him whom my soul loves; I sought him but I did not find him"</span> etc.; <span class="v">"the watchmen found me"</span> — Rashi explains: Moshe and Aharon etc.; <span class="v">"scarcely had I passed from them"</span> — Rashi explains: near to their departure from me, toward the end of the forty years — meaning shortly after they passed away — <span class="v">"until I found"</span> — meaning that He was with me in the days of Yehoshua, to conquer thirty-one kings; <span class="v">"I seized him and would not let him go"</span> etc. — see there in the commentary of Rashi. Meaning, as mentioned above: specifically after the passing of the great true <span class="h">tzadik</span>, one can find Hashem — even though at first one did not merit to find Him and was rebuked, in the aspect of <span class="v">"I sought him but did not find him,"</span> as Rashi explains, see there. Nevertheless, after his passing specifically, one can find Him — in the aspect of <span class="v">"scarcely had I passed from them, until I found"</span> etc. — whose meaning is: after their passing, as mentioned above. Meaning, as explained above: specifically through the power of the passing of the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, who merited such a <span class="h">bitul</span> in order to sweeten the intense severity of the harsh judgments from all of Israel — which could not be nullified through any <span class="h">bitul</span> toward <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, only through his actual passing. And then specifically the judgment is sweetened, and one can draw close to Hashem specifically through the power of his passing. For the <span class="h">tzadik</span> passed away specifically for this: so that he would complete through his death what he could not complete in his lifetime — like Moshe, Aharon and Dovid, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 22                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 22</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"Hashem sat [enthroned] at the Flood"</span> — which is said regarding the Giving of the Torah, as mentioned above. For the entire Giving of the Torah is drawn from the aspect of the above: from the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, which is drawn through the intensity of judgment, G-d forbid. Therefore in truth, at the time of the Giving of the Torah there was an intensity of judgment, as our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="mx">"the Holy One blessed be He wished to return the world to <i>tohu vavohu</i> [void and emptiness]."</span> And this is the aspect of: <span class="mx">"the Torah was given from the mouth of <i>G'vurah</i> [Might/Judgment]"</span> — from the mouth of <span class="h">G'vurah</span> specifically. And this is the aspect of <span class="mx">"He held the mountain over them like a barrel"</span> etc. — meaning the great judgment was aroused then, the aspect of the great judgment of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>. And Israel were exceedingly holy then and merited at the time of the intensity of the judgment to nullify themselves completely in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>. And then, through the <span class="h">bitul</span>, they merited to draw the Torah from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, from which all Torah derives, as mentioned above — which is the aspect of the sound of the <span class="h">shofar</span>, the aspect of the <span class="h">shofar</span> of the receiving of the Torah etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of what our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="mx">"with every utterance that went forth from the mouth of the Holy One blessed be He, their souls departed, and the Holy One blessed be He brought upon them rains of blessing and revived them,"</span> as it says: <span class="v">"a rain of generosity You poured down, O G-d."</span> Meaning, as mentioned above: that their souls departed with every utterance — this is the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above. And they merited such a <span class="h">bitul</span> that they passed away through it. But Hashem had mercy on them and brought upon them rains of blessing until they returned from there in the aspect of <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>. And from there they received the entire Torah — which is the aspect of the waters of <span class="h">da'as</span>, the aspect of the rains of blessing drawn from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> when returning from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 23                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 23</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="h">sukkah</span>. The aspect of the Clouds of Glory that are drawn from the light of the <span class="h">bitul</span> of <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, of which it is said: <span class="v">"he shall not enter at all times into the Holy... for in a cloud I shall appear"</span> etc.; <span class="v">"with this shall Aharon enter the Holy."</span> And from this holy cloud that is upon the <span class="h">Kapores</span> [Cover of the Ark], where the <span class="h">Kohen Gadol</span> enters on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> — from there the aspect of <span class="h">sukkah</span> is drawn, the aspect of the Clouds of Glory that Israel merits to enter after <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> through the holy <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">sukkah</span>, which is the aspect of the Clouds of Glory, as explained in the words of our Sages of blessed memory.</div>

<div class="p">For the cloud upon the <span class="h">Kapores</span> — from there Hashem spoke with Moshe. And from there he received the entire holy Torah. As it is written: <span class="v">"and when Moshe came to the Tent of Meeting... he heard the Voice... from between the two <i>K'ruvim</i>, and He spoke to him."</span> For this holy cloud is the aspect of: <span class="v">"He made darkness His concealment; around Him was His shelter: the darkness of waters"</span> etc. For because the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> is impossible to receive, due to excessive light, therefore Hashem in His kindness concealed His great and awesome light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> within the cloud and darkness, in the aspect of <span class="v">"He made darkness His concealment"</span> — so that through this cloud and darkness the light would be contracted, so that one could receive it gradually and in measure. And this cloud and darkness is the aspect of a guard before the great, infinite light, so that one who is not fit for it should not approach.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Hashem warned Aharon: <span class="v">"he shall not enter at all times into the Holy... lest he die, for in a cloud I shall appear upon the <i>Kapores</i>."</span> And Rashi explains: lest he die as his sons died, who entered the innermost sanctum without permission. Therefore the <span class="h">Sitra d'Mosa</span> [aspect of death] ruled over them — drawn and chained from this cloud and darkness, from which the aspect of judgments derives, when one does not know how to enter there. For the <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span> mentioned above — this is the aspect of the <span class="mx">four who entered the <i>Pardes</i></span>: they entered where they entered and wished to be included and gaze at the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="h">tachlis</span>. But not all of them were fit for this. Therefore not all of them emerged in peace: <span class="mx">"this one gazed and was struck; this one gazed and died."</span> And another cut the plantings completely. Only R' Akiva alone <span class="mx">"entered in peace and emerged in peace,"</span> for Hashem Himself saved him, responding to the ministering angels: <span class="mx">"this elder is worthy to use My glory."</span></div>

<div class="p">For one who merits to enter there with permission, like R' Akiva and the like — then this cloud and darkness is a great good for him. For through this cloud and darkness that surrounds the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, as mentioned above — through this specifically one merits to be included and to gaze at the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> without being nullified there from existence. And then one merits, when returning from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, that the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> remains for him. For the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> is imprinted as a holy <span class="h">reshimah</span> within this holy cloud — there specifically the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> is imaged and imprinted, specifically because of the cloud and darkness, which is the aspect of contraction. For without it, the light could not be imaged at all. Therefore all prophecies and all attainments of all the prophets and sages are received specifically through this holy cloud, as it is written: <span class="v">"in a pillar of cloud He spoke to them."</span> For there specifically the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> is imaged and imprinted, from which all their attainment derives. For from there is the essential receiving of Torah and prophecy, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And the holy people of Israel, in their entirety, merit on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> to be included and nullified in the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, through the <span class="h">Kohen Gadol</span> who enters on their behalf into the innermost sanctum etc., as mentioned above. And then, from the light of the holy <span class="h">reshimah</span> that is imaged within the holy cloud mentioned above, they merit the aspect of the receiving of the Tablets on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> — which is the generality of the Torah, as mentioned above. And then, after <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, they merit that this holy cloud should be drawn upon all of Israel — and this is the aspect of <span class="h">sukkah</span>, which is the aspect of the Clouds of Glory drawn from there. And through these Clouds of Glory — the aspect of <span class="h">sukkah</span> — through this one draws upon them the light of the attainment of Torah from the light of the holy <span class="h">reshimah</span>, throughout all the seven days of <span class="h">Sukkos</span>. Until one merits on <span class="h">Shemini Atzeres</span> and <span class="h">Simchas Torah</span> to receive the attainment of Torah in perfection: to complete it and begin it and rejoice in its joy. For then one merits the aspect of receiving the Torah in perfection — through having merited throughout all the seven days of <span class="h">Sukkah</span> to receive the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, which is the root of the Torah, through the Clouds of Glory drawn upon us through the holy <span class="h">sukkah</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the seven holy <span class="h">Ushpizin Ila'in Kadishin</span> [Exalted Holy Guests] — they are the Seven Shepherds who enter the <span class="h">sukkah</span>: Avraham, Yitzchak, Yaakov etc. For these Seven Shepherds are the aspect of the entirety of all the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of every generation, through whom the essential drawing of the revelation of His G-dliness in the world is accomplished — drawn from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span> that the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> merit, as mentioned above. For each of the Seven Shepherds mentioned above — each one repaired and drew and revealed one holy attribute of His attributes, drawn from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above, through which attributes the essential revelation of His G-dliness takes place. For in the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, it is above all the attributes and all the <span class="h">Sefiros</span>, as mentioned above. Only when returning from the <span class="h">bitul</span> in the aspect of <span class="h">v'shov</span> does one receive the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, as mentioned above. From there the revelation of the attributes and <span class="h">Sefiros</span> begins — which are the generality of all the Torah, the generality of all the holy Names, the entirety of all the worlds, through which the essential revelation of His G-dliness takes place, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And the holy <span class="h">Avos</span> — each one merited the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>, and then merited afterward, through the <span class="h">reshimah</span> drawn when returning from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, to repair and reveal one attribute of His attributes. Meaning: Avraham repaired and revealed, through the light of his <span class="h">reshimah</span> drawn through his <span class="h">bitul</span>, the aspect of the attribute of <span class="h">Chesed</span>. And likewise Yitzchak — the attribute of <span class="h">Pachad</span> [Dread]. And likewise Yaakov — the attribute of <span class="h">Tiferes</span> etc. For all of these seven attributes — which are the generality of everything, as mentioned above — all are drawn from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> that the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> mentioned above drew through the aspect of their <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore on the holy <span class="h">Chag HaSukkos</span>, when we are engaged in drawing upon us the light of the holy <span class="h">reshimah</span> through the Clouds of Glory — this <span class="h">reshimah</span> that illuminates upon us through the <span class="h">bitul</span> we merited on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> — therefore then all the Seven Shepherds enter our holy <span class="h">sukkah</span>, which is the aspect of the Clouds of Glory. They enter it because through them is the essential drawing of the light of the holy <span class="h">reshimah</span>, through which the essential revelation of His G-dliness was revealed in the world through them, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore they are called "Shepherds." For these awesome <span class="h">tzadikim</span> are themselves the aspect of the Masters of the Field who are engaged in the tikun of the Supernal Field mentioned above — whose essential tikun is through the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> and the gazing toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, as understood from the Torah mentioned above. Therefore they are called Shepherds. For they are in the aspect of shepherds within the Supernal Field. For just as the physical shepherd — his entire occupation and work is within the field — so too these <span class="h">tzadikim</span> are the aspect of shepherds within the Supernal Field. For they are the aspect of the Masters of the Field who are engaged in the tikun of the Supernal Field through the <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore in truth, all the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> mentioned above were literal shepherds in the field. For it is known that the lower field is a pattern of the Supernal Field, and all the grasses and plants and herbage of the field are all sparks of supernal souls. And the <span class="h">Avos</span>, while shepherding in the field, were repairing the Field at its supernal root. For they are the masters of the Supernal Field, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 24                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 24</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the Four Species taken on <span class="h">Sukkos</span>. And with them we rejoice before Him, as it is written: <span class="v">"you shall take for yourselves on the first day the fruit of a beautiful tree"</span> etc.; <span class="v">"and you shall rejoice before Hashem your G-d"</span> etc. For now we display our power — what we accomplished on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> and <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>: that through all the tikunim mentioned above we merited to repair prayer, meaning to make one unity out of the entire prayer. All of this is the aspect of the tikun of the Supernal Field mentioned above, as explained there: when a person stands to pray, he walks in the Supernal Field and gathers blossoms, grasses and beautiful flowers etc. — see there and what emerges from there. For as long as one does not merit to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> — which is the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above — the prayer is not repaired in perfection, for the utterance does not allow the soul to proceed further and gather grasses and flowers and wondrous plants from the Field etc. And the essential tikun is through gazing upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> etc. — through which one makes one unity out of the entire prayer, and then specifically one merits prayer in perfection. And then one can walk in one's prayer in the Supernal Field and gather roses and wondrous plants through the utterances of holy prayer etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the holy and awesome Four Species — all of which are types of plants that the holy Torah commanded us to take on the days of <span class="h">Chag HaSukkos</span>, these Four Species from the plants of the field. They are the aspect of the entirety of prayer, the entirety of the holy souls, which are the aspect of the herbage of the field. And in that we take these Four Species from the plants of the field — in this we display the greatness of our power, what we accomplished on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> and <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>: that through the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above we merited to repair prayer, until we can gather and collect in our hands wondrous plants from the Supernal Field. This is the aspect of all the Four Species, which are the entirety of all the holy plants of the Field. For these Four Species include all the souls that grow in the Field mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">As our Sages of blessed memory said: the Four Species are the aspect of all the categories that exist among the souls of Israel. They are the aspect of <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who possess Torah and good deeds — they are the aspect of those with both fragrance and taste, the aspect of the <span class="h">esrog</span>. And there are those with fragrance but not taste — the aspect of the <span class="h">hadas</span> [myrtle]. And those with taste but not fragrance — the aspect of the <span class="h">lulav</span>, palm branch. And there are those with neither taste nor fragrance — the transgressors of Israel, the aspect of the <span class="h">arvei nachal</span> [willows of the brook]. It emerges that the Four Species include all the categories of all of Israel, which are the generality of all the souls. Therefore they are all plants of the field. For all the souls are the aspect of plants of the Supernal Field, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And through taking on <span class="h">Sukkos</span> these Four Species from the plants of the field — in this we display the greatness of our power, what we accomplished on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> and <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>: through the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> etc. as mentioned above, we merited to repair prayer, until we merit to gather in our hands the entirety of all the wondrous plants of the Field that are gathered and collected when one merits prayer in perfection — which is the aspect of these holy Four Species, all of which are the aspect of the plants of the Supernal Field, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of what is found in the holy Zohar: <span class="mx">"who is victorious? One who holds the weapons of war in his hands"</span> — which are the holy Four Species. For the essential victory of the war that we won on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> is recognized and revealed through the holy Four Species, which are the aspect of the entirety of the plants of the Supernal Field — which is the essential aspect of the tikun of prayer that we accomplished through the <span class="h">bitul</span> of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> and <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 25                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 25</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential taking of the Four Species is during <span class="h">Hallel</span>. For they are the aspect of the tikun of prayer — which is the aspect of <span class="h">Hallel</span>, which is the entirety of prayer. For <span class="h">Hallel</span> is the entirety of prayer and all songs and praises, as Rashi explains: <span class="v">"I shall relate all Your wonders"</span> — this is <span class="h">Hallel</span>, which contains [reference to] the past etc. and the future etc. For all the Four Species are the aspect of the wondrous grasses gathered from the Supernal Field through the tikun of prayer, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of why one waves [the <span class="h">lulav</span>] during <span class="v">"Hodu LaHashem"</span> at the beginning and end. For <span class="v">"Hodu"</span> at the beginning and end — this is the aspect of the tikun of prayer, whose essential tikun is when one merits to make one unity out of the entire prayer, until even when standing at the end of prayer, one is still standing at the first word of the prayer. This is the aspect of <span class="v">"Hodu LaHashem"</span> at the beginning and end. For even when standing at the end, one is still standing at the first word, which is <span class="v">"Hodu."</span> For it is entirely one, as mentioned above. And there is the essential place of the wavings of the <span class="h">lulav</span> and its species. For the wavings are the aspect of <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span> — arousing the light of <span class="h">da'as</span> drawn from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, through the wavings, as is known from the Writings [of the Ari"zal]. For the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span> is drawn through the aspect of <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore through the wavings of the <span class="h">lulav</span> — which are the aspect of <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span> — one arouses this light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>. Through this is the essential tikun of prayer: to make one unity out of the entire prayer, so that at the last word one is still standing at the first word — which is the aspect of <span class="v">"Hodu LaHashem"</span> at the beginning and end. Therefore one waves there with the Four Species, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 26                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 26</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Simchas Bais HaSho'aivah</span> [the Celebration of the Water Drawing] on <span class="h">Sukkos</span>. Therefore then is the time of our joy. For the essential drawing of the waters of <span class="h">da'as</span> from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above is through joy — which is the vessel for receiving the Torah, as mentioned above in the Torah mentioned above — see there.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 27                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 27</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one takes the Four Species specifically in one's hands. This is the aspect of: <span class="mx">"each one points with his finger"</span> specifically — which is the aspect of the Torah drawn from the light of the shining of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="v">"finger of G-d,"</span> as explained at the end of the Torah mentioned above. And in this we display the greatness of our power — what we, Israel the holy people, accomplished through the holiness of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> and <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, which are in the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> etc. as mentioned above. Meaning: we accomplished that through this we merit to grasp Him, as it were, literally in our hands. As explained in the holy Zohar: through taking the Four Species we take Him, as it were — see there. Meaning: through taking the Four Species in our hands we draw upon us the light of the holy <span class="h">reshimah</span>, until it is imaged and imprinted in our hands literally, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"set me as a seal upon your heart, as a seal upon your arm"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">And in this we display the greatness of the power of Israel. For Hashem is exalted and elevated beyond all the thoughts of all the <span class="h">Chayos</span> and <span class="h">Serafim</span> and <span class="h">Ofanei HaKodesh</span>, and beyond all the spirituality of all the worlds — for no thought grasps Him at all. And Israel the holy people merits to draw His G-dliness literally into their hands through taking the Four Species — through which they take Him, as it were, literally in their hands, as is understood from the holy Zohar, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">For the light of the revelation of His G-dliness is exceedingly exalted and lofty. Therefore it is impossible to receive even the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> except through many vessels etc., as mentioned above. Therefore one must guard the remembrance exceedingly, to remember well the light of this <span class="h">reshimah</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore one must guard it and bind it in the aspect of a seal within a seal — the aspect of <span class="v">"set me as a seal upon your heart, as a seal upon your arm, for love is as strong as death"</span> etc. Meaning: the love and yearning to be included in the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> is so strong that one could die and depart through it, on account of the excess of light, as mentioned above. Therefore one must contract the light through many contractions and vessels, and bind the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above with two seals, in the aspect of a seal within a seal — which is the aspect of the two seals that we are engaged in repairing from <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> until <span class="h">Hoshana Rabbah</span>, as is known.</div>

<div class="p">For first one repairs one seal — the aspect of <span class="v">"as a seal upon your heart"</span> — meaning one binds the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> within one's heart. For as long as one does not draw the <span class="h">da'as</span> — which is the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> — into the heart, and the <span class="h">da'as</span> remains only in the mind, one does not yet know of Him at all. For the essential <span class="h">da'as</span> is when it is drawn into the heart, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"and you shall know today and bring it into your heart"</span> etc. — as explained in the words of Rabbainu of blessed memory many times. This is the aspect of the seal of the heart — the aspect of <span class="v">"as a seal upon your heart"</span> etc. But even though one binds the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> in the heart, one still fears that the light might depart, G-d forbid, due to the greatness of its light. Therefore one must draw yet another seal upon seal — the aspect of <span class="v">"as a seal upon your heart, as a seal upon your arm."</span> We repair yet another seal upon the seal of the heart mentioned above. For we draw His G-dliness literally into our hands through taking the Four Species in our hands — which is the aspect of <span class="v">"as a seal upon your arm."</span> In order to bind well the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above, so that we will never forget Him, forever and ever.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 28                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 28</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Hoshana Rabbah</span>. For we are engaged in this tikun throughout all the days of <span class="h">Sukkos</span>: to draw the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> and to receive from there the attainment of Torah and to repair prayer etc. — through the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">sukkah</span> and the Four Species, as mentioned above. And on each day of the seven days of <span class="h">Sukkos</span>, the light of the holy <span class="h">reshimah</span> is drawn upon us from one of the Seven Shepherds. Through this, the attribute of that <span class="h">tzadik</span> — which he repaired through his <span class="h">bitul</span> etc. as mentioned above — is repaired and revealed. Meaning: on the first day, the attribute of <span class="h">Chesed</span>, which is the attribute of Avraham. And so on the second day and the third day etc., as is brought in the Kavanos.</div>

<div class="p">Until one arrives at the seventh day, which is the aspect of Dovid. And then the tikun of prayer is completed — which is the aspect of Dovid, and this is the essential tikun. For our entire occupation is to repair prayer, to make one unity out of the entire prayer, so that we can speak many prayers, songs and praises — which is the aspect of Dovid, who is the <span class="v">"sweet singer of Israel,"</span> in whose name all the prayers, songs and praises are called. As our Sages of blessed memory said, and as explained at the end of the Torah mentioned above on the verse: <span class="v">"Boaz said to Rus"</span> — that Rus is the aspect of the <span class="h">nefesh</span> from which the utterances of prayer emerge. And he brought proof from what our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="mx">"why was her name called Rus? Because from her descended Dovid, who <i>rivah</i> [saturated/drenched] the Holy One blessed be He with songs and praises."</span></div>

<div class="p">It emerges that Dovid is the aspect of the tikun of prayer mentioned above. For Dovid is the seventh Shepherd, for in his days the tikun mentioned above was completed — until he merited such a <span class="h">bitul</span> that he repaired prayer in the aspect mentioned above, meaning he was able to make one unity out of the entire prayer. Through this one can speak many prayers, songs and praises, as mentioned above. Therefore Dovid truly multiplied prayers, songs and praises more than all, and saturated the Holy One blessed be He with songs and praises. For he merited the tikun of prayer, through which one can speak abundantly, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Hoshana Rabbah</span>, which is the aspect of Dovid — for then specifically the tikun is completed. For the essential tikun is the aspect of the tikun of prayer, repaired through the tikun of the <span class="h">bitul</span> etc. as mentioned above — which is the aspect of Dovid, as mentioned above. Therefore on <span class="h">Hoshana Rabbah</span> the aspect of the tikun of the seal within the seal is completed, for then is the aspect of Dovid, through whom the tikun is completed, as mentioned above. Therefore Dovid specifically merited to find the place of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>. For in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> is the essential tikun of prayer, as it is written: <span class="v">"for My house is a house of prayer"</span> — for all prayers ascend through there. For there is the <span class="h">Kodesh Hakodashim</span>, where the essential perfection of the <span class="h">bitul</span> in the innermost sanctum resides etc. as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of Dovid, who merited the tikun of prayer in perfection, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one stays awake on the night of <span class="h">Hoshana Rabbah</span>. For then one must complete the tikun mentioned above — meaning to draw and bind well the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> in our hearts and in our hands, as mentioned above. Therefore one must banish sleep from one's eyes, for the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> is drawn through the aspect of the awakening of sleep, as explained above regarding the <span class="h">shofar</span>, which is the aspect of the awakening of sleep, through which one draws the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> etc. as mentioned above. And now, at the time of the completion of the seal within the seal on <span class="h">Hoshana Rabbah</span>, one must banish sleep entirely, in order to draw the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> in perfection — which is the aspect of the awakening of sleep, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is what is written in the holy Zohar: <span class="mx">"on that day — 'Yitzchak sat and dug the wells of water'"</span> etc. For on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, through the <span class="h">bitul</span> in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, one digs wells of water — meaning one draws the waters of <span class="h">da'as</span> and the Torah drawn from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> — which is the aspect of the <span class="h">shofar</span>, the aspect of receiving the Torah, as mentioned above. But then the Torah has not yet been revealed in actuality, for it is impossible to repair all at once, as explained above at length. And now, on <span class="h">Hoshana Rabbah</span>, which is the completion of the tikun, one goes back and digs the wells of water. For then the waters of <span class="h">da'as</span> and Torah are drawn in actuality — until one merits to make <span class="h">Simchas Torah</span>: to complete and begin the Torah and to rejoice in its joy. For then, after the completion of the tikun through all the <span class="h">mitzvos</span> mentioned above in this holy month, one merits to draw the aspect of receiving the Torah in perfection, in actual reality — and this is the aspect of <span class="h">Simchas Torah</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--           TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY — clearly demarcated          -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="ts" style="margin-top:3rem;">
<div class="sn" style="font-size:1.2rem;text-align:center;border-bottom:2px solid var(--accent);">— Translator's Summary —</div>
</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 18–19: Flood = Excessive Light; the Rainbow = the Reshimah</div>

<div class="p">The Flood waters were the waters of da'as/Torah that became destructive because the generation had no vessels. "Hashem sat at the Flood" (Tehilim 29) is said regarding Matan Torah — same waters, opposite outcome. Had the generation repented, the waters would have been "rains of blessing and generosity" (= Torah at Sinai). Without teshuvah: the waters of da'as become a Flood. After the Flood, Noach's offerings earned the rainbow — which is the luminous colors of the reshimah of the bitul. The rainbow's sign = "to remember" = zikaron = Rosh HaShanah. The shofar's mnemonic letters = Kuf-Shin-Tav = Keshes (rainbow). The reddening face of the shofar-blower = the light of the reshimah.</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 20–21: The Passing of the Tzadik; Dovid and the Bais HaMikdash; Moshe and Eretz Yisra'el</div>

<div class="p">Sometimes judgment is so intense that the tzadik must pass away to sweeten it. Then: the orphaned generation draws light from his reshimah. Mesushelach's death before the Flood = this pattern. "The righteous one has perished and no man takes it to heart" — the generation must understand that the tzadik passed because of their sins. Every tzadik leaves a reshimah; when the generation does teshuvah, they draw great light from it. Dovid wanted to build the Bais HaMikdash (= site of ultimate bitul, Kodesh Hakodashim) but was told: only after your passing. "The righteous are greater in death than in life." Moshe wanted to enter Eretz Yisra'el — but this too required his passing. Yehoshua conquered it through Moshe's power, activated only by the histalkus (ratzo without shov = ultimate bitul). Shir HaShirim: "I sought him but did not find him... scarcely had I passed from them, until I found" = specifically after the tzadik's passing, Hashem is found.</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 22–23: Matan Torah = Bitul with Rains of Blessing; the Sukkah = the Cloud</div>

<div class="p">At Sinai, intense judgment ("He wished to return the world to tohu vavohu"; "Torah given from G'vurah"; "held the mountain like a barrel"). Israel merited ultimate bitul — souls departed with each utterance, then revived with rains of blessing = Torah from the reshimah. The sukkah = the Clouds of Glory = the cloud on the Kapores in the Kodesh Hakodashim. The cloud = Hashem's concealment of His Ain Sof light (necessary to prevent excessive light / Breaking of Vessels). Aharon's sons died entering without permission (= the four who entered Pardes — only R' Akiva emerged in peace). The cloud is the medium through which the reshimah is imprinted — all prophecy and Torah come through this cloud. After Yom Kippur, this cloud extends over all Israel = the sukkah. Seven days of Sukkos = receiving the reshimah through the seven Ushpizin (Shepherds) who each revealed one Middah. They are the Ba'alei HaSadeh — literally shepherds in the field, repairing the Supernal Field.</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 24–25: The Four Species = Plants of the Supernal Field; Wavings = Ratzo v'Shov</div>

<div class="p">Taking the Four Species displays what we accomplished on Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur: repairing prayer until we can gather wondrous plants from the Supernal Field. The Four Species = all categories of Jewish souls (esrog = Torah + deeds; hadas = fragrance without taste; lulav = taste without fragrance; aravah = neither). Taking them in hand = victory display. "Who is victorious? One who holds the weapons in his hands" (Zohar). Wavings during "Hodu" at beginning and end of Hallel = ratzo v'shov = arousing the reshimah. Standing at the end yet still at the first word "Hodu" = the unity of prayer.</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 26–28: Simchas Bais HaSho'aivah, Two Seals, Hoshana Rabbah, Simchas Torah</div>

<div class="p">Simchas Bais HaSho'aivah = joy = vessel for receiving Torah. Taking the Four Species in hand = "set me as a seal upon your arm" — the second seal. First seal = heart (da'as drawn into the heart, per Rabbainu); second seal = arm/hand (Four Species). Two seals needed because the light is so intense it could depart. Hoshana Rabbah = Dovid = the seventh Shepherd = completion of the tikun of prayer. "Sweet singer of Israel." Rus = nefesh from which prayer emerges; Dovid "saturated" Hashem with songs. Dovid found the place of the Bais HaMikdash = essential tikun of prayer = "My house is a house of prayer." Staying awake on Hoshana Rabbah night = awakening of sleep = drawing the reshimah. "Yitzchak dug wells of water" = on Rosh HaShanah the wells are dug; on Hoshana Rabbah the water flows in actuality. This leads to Simchas Torah: completing and beginning the Torah, receiving Torah in perfection.</div>

